  "claims": {
    "C1": "A phone shows its position on a map without transmitting to satellites (receive-only).",
    "C2": "GNSS position is computed by the receiver from signals broadcast by satellites.",
    "C3": "Position fix = intersection of distances to known points (trilateration; 3 distances in 2D, 4 unknowns in 3D+time).",
    "C4": "GPS satellites orbit at ~20,200 km altitude, ~12 h period; positions are tracked and uploaded by ground control.",
    "C5": "Signals travel at the speed of light; ~20,000 km range ~ 0.07 s; 1 ns ~ 30 cm.",
    "C6": "Constellation of 24+ satellites in 6 planes so that >=4 are visible nearly everywhere.",
    "C7": "Each satellite broadcasts a PRN code + navigation message (ephemeris, time); receiver correlates code to get travel time.",
    "C8": "Satellites carry atomic clocks; receivers use inexpensive quartz clocks.",
    "C9": "The receiver clock offset is a fourth unknown solved with a 4th satellite; receivers also obtain precise time.",
    "C10": "GNSS time is used beyond navigation (e.g. network and financial timestamps).",
    "C11": "Special relativity (~ -7 us/day) and general relativity (~ +45 us/day) give a net ~ +38 us/day on satellite clocks.",
    "C12": "Uncorrected, 38 us/day would accumulate ~10 km/day of ranging error; satellite clock frequency is pre-adjusted.",
    "C13": "Residual errors: ionosphere/troposphere delay, multipath, receiver noise; typical consumer accuracy metres.",
    "C14": "Augmentation/dual-frequency and other GNSS (Galileo, GLONASS, BeiDou) improve robustness."
  },
